AU  - Anjos, Júlia Silva Fonseca dos
AU  - Barra, Isabel Pires
AU  - Costa, Edilma de Oliveira
AU  - Mendonça, Ana Elza de Oliveira
T2  - Rev. Enferm. UFSM
AB  - Abstract  Objective: to analyze fake news about COVID-19 published on the fact-checking website "Aos Fatos".  Method: descriptive cross-sectional research, carried out with data published from January to December 2020, on the fact-checking website "Aos Fatos". The collection took place from January 25 to 29, 2021 and the sample consisted of 205 fake news, submitted to absolute and relative frequency analysis.  Results: the news was about the severity of COVID-19 (27.3%), the vaccines under development (20%) and the choice of drugs (13.7%).  Conclusions: the sharing of false information about COVID-19 contributed to disbelief in science, which may have compromised adherence to official prevention measures recommended in Brazil and negatively influenced the adherence of the population. Circulating fake news must be monitored and refuted for genuine information to reach the public.
